I will implement a debounce function that delays invoking the provided function until after a specified number of milliseconds have elapsed since the last call. The returned object will expose a `call` method to trigger the debounced function and a `flush` method to immediately execute any pending invocation. Edge cases to consider include handling multiple rapid calls, ensuring the timer is cleared and reset correctly, and guaranteeing that `flush` runs the most recent pending call even if it has been waiting.

ANSWER:
```javascript
function debounceCount(fn, ms) {
  let timer = null;
  let pendingArgs = null;

  const call = function (...args) {
    pendingArgs = args;
    if (timer) clearTimeout(timer);
    timer = setTimeout(() => {
      fn.apply(this, pendingArgs);
      timer = null;
      pendingArgs = null;
    }, ms);
  };

  const flush = function () {
    if (timer) {
      clearTimeout(timer);
      if (pendingArgs) {
        fn.apply(this, pendingArgs);
        pendingArgs = null;
      }
      timer = null;
    }
  };

  return { call, flush };
}

module.exports = debounceCount;
```

