I will implement `parseQuery` by splitting the query string on '&' to get key‑value pairs, then decoding each key and value with `decodeURIComponent`. For each pair I will check if the key already exists in the result object; if it does, I will convert the existing value to an array (or push the new value) to handle repeated keys, otherwise I will assign the value directly. This approach correctly handles percent‑encoded characters and the edge case where a key appears multiple times.

ANSWER:
```javascript
function parseQuery(qs) {
  const result = {};
  if (!qs) return result;

  const pairs = qs.split('&');
  for (const pair of pairs) {
    if (!pair) continue;
    const [key, value = ''] = pair.split('=');
    const decodedKey = decodeURIComponent(key.replace(/\+/g, ' '));
    const decodedValue = decodeURIComponent(value.replace(/\+/g, ' '));

    if (result.hasOwnProperty(decodedKey)) {
      const existing = result[decodedKey];
      result[decodedKey] = Array.isArray(existing)
        ? [...existing, decodedValue]
        : [existing, decodedValue];
    } else {
      result[decodedKey] = decodedValue;
    }
  }
  return result;
}

module.exports = { parseQuery };
```

