I will implement a function that takes an array and an array of key‑extraction functions, then returns a new array sorted by the first key, breaking ties with the second key, and so on. The implementation will use `Array.prototype.map` to create a copy of each element with its computed keys, then `Array.prototype.sort` with a comparator that iterates over the key functions in order, handling `undefined` values by treating them as lower than any defined value. This approach ensures the original array is not mutated and works for any number of key functions.

ANSWER:
```javascript
function sortByMulti(arr, keyFns) {
  // Create a shallow copy of the array to avoid mutating the input
  const copy = arr.slice();

  // Map each element to an object containing the original element and its computed keys
  const withKeys = copy.map((item, index) => ({
    item,
    keys: keyFns.map(fn => fn(item, index))
  }));

  // Sort based on the priority order of key functions
  withKeys.sort((a, b) => {
    for (let i = 0; i < a.keys.length; i++) {
      const valA = a.keys[i];
      const valB = b.keys[i];

      // If both values are undefined, continue to the next key
      if (valA === undefined && valB === undefined) continue;

      // If only one is undefined, treat undefined as lower than any defined value
      if (valA === undefined) return -1;
      if (valB === undefined) return 1;

      // Compare the values (assuming they are comparable)
      if (valA < valB) return -1;
      if (valA > valB) return 1;
    }
    return 0;
  });

  // Extract the sorted original items
  return withKeys.map(entry => entry.item);
}

module.exports = sortByMulti;
