I'll create a React Native component that uses `useMemo` to filter a list of items based on a search term. The component will maintain a list of items and a search term, and the filtered list will only be recomputed when either the items or the search term changes. This approach prevents unnecessary recalculations on each render, improving performance.

One edge case to consider is handling empty or null values in the items array, which could cause errors during filtering. Another consideration is ensuring the search term comparison is case-insensitive for a better user experience.

ANSWER:
```jsx
import React, { useState, useMemo } from 'react';
import { View, TextInput, FlatList, Text } from 'react-native';

const FilteredList = () => {
  const [items] = useState([
    { id: '1', name: 'Apple' },
    { id: '2', name: 'Banana' },
    { id: '3', name: 'Cherry' },
    { id: '4', name: 'Date' },
  ]);
  const [searchTerm, setSearchTerm] = useState('');

  const filteredItems = useMemo(() => {
    if (!searchTerm) return items;
    return items.filter(item =>
      item.name.toLowerCase().includes(searchTerm.toLowerCase())
    );
  }, [items, searchTerm]);

  return (
    <View style={{ flex: 1, padding: 20 }}>
      <TextInput
        placeholder="Search fruits..."
        value={searchTerm}
        onChangeText={setSearchTerm}
        style={{
          borderWidth: 1,
          borderColor: '#ccc',
          padding: 10,
          marginBottom: 20,
          borderRadius: 5,
        }}
      />
      <FlatList
        data={filteredItems}
        keyExtractor={item => item.id}
        renderItem={({ item }) => (
          <Text style={{ padding: 10, fontSize: 18 }}>{item.name}</Text>
        )}
      />
    </View>
  );
};

export default FilteredList;
```

